We present a relativistic first-principles investigation of the Yu--Shiba--Rusinov (YSR) states associated with a single Mn adatom and Mn clusters on the Nb(110) surface. Our analysis reveals that the YSR states for the Mn adatom are predominantly localized within the Mn 3d orbitals, whereas those for the Mn clusters exhibit a more delocalized character. Furthermore, we demonstrate that the YSR states for the Mn adatom are highly sensitive to the orientation of the Mn magnetic moment relative to the surface normal. Finally, we show that the YSR states for the Mn clusters are significantly influenced by the specific cluster geometry. These findings are derived from the relativistic first-principles calculations of the spin-dependent electronic structure of the Mn adatom and Mn clusters on Nb(110).
